WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
It's very enjoyable to be able to
participate in the culture to that

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.130
extent in the target language.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:10.680
Hi there Steve Kaufmann here again, and
today I want to talk to you about culture.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:17.790
This is the seventh and last of my
personal goals in language learning.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.810
Remember if you enjoy these videos,
please subscribe, click on the

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.120
bell on the bell for notifications.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:29.460
And if you enjoy my podcasts,
please leave a review.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:31.560
We do appreciate it.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:32.189
All right.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.270
So I've been going through the
sort of seven personal goals when

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:40.950
it comes to language learning as
in sort of a hierarchical order.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:45.030
Uh, my first goal is to learn as
many words as I can, and that remains

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.129
a goal: increasing my vocabulary.

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:51.930
Then I want to have that vocabulary
so that I can start reading.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.500
It's kind of the easiest thing to
do once I can read, then I can make

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.970
more sense out of what I listened to.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:58.379
So then I increase my listening.

00:00:59.035 --> 00:01:04.795
Um, with the sort of listening, I develop
some momentum, which gets me to speaking.

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:09.385
When I speak, I realize the
gaps in my grammar and my usage.

00:01:09.385 --> 00:01:12.715
So then I focus in on grammar
sort of in that order.

00:01:12.865 --> 00:01:14.695
I don't start with grammar, for example.

00:01:15.025 --> 00:01:19.015
Then in my last video, I talked about
writing, which can be a powerful way of

00:01:19.015 --> 00:01:21.795
improving our skills in the language.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.770
Um, but it's not something that I've
been doing a lot of recently, but I

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.680
still recommend it, it's an important
way of improving in the language.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.710
And then this all brings us to
the culture, which is an important

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.510
motivator for me, because I want...

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:41.570
the reason I learned these languages is
to get closer to the culture of those

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:46.634
languages, to learn more about them, for
those countries and people and cultures

00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:52.065
to come alive for me, which they do when
I have an understanding, even an imperfect

00:01:52.065 --> 00:01:53.625
understanding, of those languages.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:57.945
Ideally, I want to approach those cultures
in the language that I'm learning.

00:01:58.544 --> 00:02:05.535
So sometimes if at an early stage in
learning the language I get too much

00:02:05.595 --> 00:02:14.160
explanation ofthe culture, of festivals,
of special names for relatives or any

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.840
of this kind of detail, uh, in English
or a language that I speak well,

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.900
uh, I find it difficult to absorb.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.110
Uh, it's not so meaningful to me.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:29.280
That's why I resist it because once
I've had enough exposure to the language

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.640
where I've been in situations, or I've
watched movies where people refer to their

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.910
um, mother's brother in certain ways.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:44.640
So these names for different family
members, or even references to, uh, you

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.840
know, culture, you know, like festivals
or, you know, in, in terms of Persian,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:54.750
uh, uh, how they eat on Nowruz and all of
this kind of thing is more meaningful when

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.330
I can access it in the target language.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.520
But of course we are dealing with
the culture from the beginning.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:03.060
Okay.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.190
When we're learning another language,
we're imitating that language.

00:03:08.399 --> 00:03:11.910
We're imitating a portion
of the culture because the

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:13.620
language is part of the culture.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:19.589
So the language is in itself, part of
the culture that we're learning, but

00:03:19.589 --> 00:03:23.940
the fine points of the culture, the
sort of, how people interact with each

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.640
other, uh, this comes alive better when
we have a better understanding of the

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.790
language and, uh, it might be through
watching a movie or a series on Netflix

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:40.859
that we come closer to a Turkish family
or Arabic, uh, you know, problems in

00:03:40.859 --> 00:03:45.120
Egypt having to do with the role of
women and these subjects that are very

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.760
often treated in, in movies or series.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.810
And if we can access them in the
target language, we sympathize

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.720
more, uh, with, with the people.

00:03:55.079 --> 00:03:57.810
So, uh, and that's very enjoyable.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.190
It's very enjoyable to be able to
participate in the culture to that

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:03.090
extent in the target language.

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.980
Similarly, when we have enough of the
language that we can be with those

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.750
people and communicate in the target
language, understand what they're saying,

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:18.360
participate in how they react with each
other, uh, participate ourselves in

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.640
the target language, that in a sense
is sort of the, the final achievement.

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:26.100
That's in a way the ultimate goal.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.159
We want to be able to do that, but
the reason I make it my seventh goal

00:04:30.159 --> 00:04:35.745
is that I feel a lot of other things
have to fall into place in order to be

00:04:35.745 --> 00:04:38.625
able to enjoy the culture in that way.

00:04:39.255 --> 00:04:43.245
That's not to say you can't, you
know, watch movies with subtitles.

00:04:43.275 --> 00:04:48.755
I mentioned, for example, that uh
watching Russian movies, like ... at

00:04:49.164 --> 00:04:53.025
an early stage in my learning, you
relying on the English subtitles.

00:04:53.205 --> 00:04:58.005
It was nevertheless, a great motivator
for me, but watching that same

00:04:58.005 --> 00:05:04.035
movie and understanding what they're
saying is a much richer experience.

00:05:04.545 --> 00:05:09.795
So as with many of the sort of
hierarchy of goals, my greater

00:05:09.855 --> 00:05:13.965
emphasis at a particular stage might
be on reading or listening or, or

00:05:13.965 --> 00:05:18.015
whatever, but there's still elements
of all of these goals at every stage.

00:05:18.525 --> 00:05:20.655
Uh, it's just a matter
of how much emphasis.

00:05:20.655 --> 00:05:25.065
So at an early stage, I may resist
a great emphasis on festivals

00:05:25.065 --> 00:05:27.255
or these kinds of things.

00:05:27.525 --> 00:05:31.944
But if I'm now at a stage, say in
Persian, where I can read like Sahra

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:36.585
did for  me on LingQ, she created
a course on Iranian history, a

00:05:36.594 --> 00:05:42.044
course on Iranian food, a course on
different minorities, Iran in Iran.

00:05:42.044 --> 00:05:46.544
If I can access all of this information
in the target language, to me,

00:05:46.544 --> 00:05:48.315
that's, that's the time to do it.

00:05:48.315 --> 00:05:49.875
So I tend to want to save it.

00:05:50.345 --> 00:05:58.950
Similarly, you know, reading novels,
um, in translation is not as attractive

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:03.600
to me as waiting until I can read
those novels in the target language.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:10.630
Uh, so I kind of save the cultural thing,
the bulk of it until I have enough of the

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:16.845
language, but I don't deny myself some
peaks at the culture at an early stage.

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:21.645
You know, there's so many more
opportunities for us to access different

00:06:21.645 --> 00:06:25.905
aspects of the culture today then was the
case before I've mentioned several times.

00:06:26.775 --> 00:06:32.085
The Jordanian cartoon series,
which is very much aimed at, at a

00:06:32.085 --> 00:06:34.335
Jordanian audience with cartoons.

00:06:34.365 --> 00:06:37.365
It's a bit like the Simpsons, but
with sort of little morals in every

00:06:37.365 --> 00:06:42.075
episode, uh family life it's called.

00:06:42.705 --> 00:06:45.735
I can access that using LingQ,
granted sentence by sentence,

00:06:45.735 --> 00:06:47.055
going through the words.

00:06:48.145 --> 00:06:52.724
Netflix enables us to access,
you know, series where we kind of

00:06:52.724 --> 00:06:54.735
find the same family every series.

00:06:54.735 --> 00:06:55.955
And they're speaking in that language.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.180
We're trying as much as we can to
understand what they're saying.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:04.350
And so there's just so many more ways we
can access the culture then was the case

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:09.780
before, and we can access that aspect of
the culture that's of interest to us far

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:15.690
too often, uh, in textbooks, the textbook
writer wants to introduce to you those

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:19.365
aspects of the culture that they think
are interesting or that you should know.

00:07:19.845 --> 00:07:24.915
Whereas I think where it's more
satisfying to access those aspects of

00:07:24.915 --> 00:07:26.505
the culture that are of interest to us.

00:07:26.685 --> 00:07:29.205
And that could be anime
in the case of Japanese.

00:07:29.505 --> 00:07:32.085
It could be a K-pop in the case of Korean.

00:07:32.445 --> 00:07:36.825
And I think as is the case with all
aspects of language learning, where it's

00:07:36.825 --> 00:07:41.535
meaningful, where you are, you are doing
things of interest to you, things that you

00:07:41.535 --> 00:07:43.735
find enjoyable you're going to do better.

00:07:44.215 --> 00:07:50.340
And so this final goal then the seventh
goal of accessing the culture is best

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:53.760
done when we take the initiative,
we choose things of interest to us.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:57.630
If we're able to access it in part
or wholly in the target language.

00:07:58.335 --> 00:08:00.195
It makes the whole
exercise more meaningful.

00:08:00.375 --> 00:08:02.055
So there you have it.

00:08:02.055 --> 00:08:06.825
This is the seventh and final video
on this subject of my language

00:08:06.825 --> 00:08:09.855
learning goals, my hierarchy
of language learning goals.

00:08:10.365 --> 00:08:16.094
So it started with accumulating
vocabulary, which remains a goal then

00:08:16.664 --> 00:08:18.975
uh, reading leads me onto listening.

00:08:19.365 --> 00:08:20.985
Listening leads me on to speaking.

00:08:21.225 --> 00:08:24.765
Speaking makes me aware of
my shortcomings and usage.

00:08:25.215 --> 00:08:30.765
Uh, from there, I want to work on my
writing in order to improve my usage.

00:08:31.065 --> 00:08:33.855
And when I have all the pieces
of the puzzle put together, then

00:08:33.855 --> 00:08:36.849
I'm able to access the culture.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:38.409
Now I did a couple of videos...

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:41.319
I have done some videos on this
subject before, and you might

00:08:41.319 --> 00:08:42.459
want to have a look at them.

00:08:42.459 --> 00:08:46.569
One was called Culture and Language
Learning, and the other one is First

00:08:46.569 --> 00:08:48.400
Learn the Language then the Culture.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:50.530
So thank you for listening.

00:08:50.589 --> 00:08:52.180
And that's the end of this series.

00:08:52.329 --> 00:08:53.110
Bye for now.

